From b236877f43b73aa6b45c8308386d0c4f4dcdb9a9 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Nick Hainke <vincent@systemli.org>
Date: Sat, 3 Oct 2026 20:40:55 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 2/3] ujail: add VRF support

Add '-v <vrf>' to bind all AF_INET/AF_INET6 sockets of the jailed
process to a Linux VRF device, like 'ip vrf exec <vrf>' does, without
depending on iproute2, libbpf or libnl.

The VRF is resolved with an RTM_GETLINK request, which also checks the
device really is a VRF. ujail then creates the cgroup "vrf/<vrf>" below
its own cgroup (procd's per-instance cgroup), attaches a
BPF_PROG_TYPE_CGROUP_SOCK program at BPF_CGROUP_INET_SOCK_CREATE which
sets sk_bound_dev_if to the VRF's ifindex, and moves the jailed process
into that cgroup. This is the same mechanism iproute2 uses.

The jailed process joins the VRF in exec_jail(), right after joining
namespaces given with -j, so the VRF is looked up in the network
namespace the process ends up in. The cgroup directory is opened before
those joins, since joining a mount namespace may replace
/sys/fs/cgroup, and the join happens before a joined user namespace
drops the privileges needed for cgroup and BPF setup.

Combinations which can't work are refused up front:
 - an OCI bundle (-J): ujail moves the process into the bundle's
   cgroup after the VRF cgroup was joined
 - a new network namespace (-N): it contains no VRF device yet
 - a new user namespace (-f) created by clone(): the process is
   unprivileged in the initial user namespace from the start

Assisted-by: Claude:claude-opus-5-5
Signed-off-by: Nick Hainke <vincent@systemli.org>
